4+8 = 28,\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* divisible by $3.$ So, $1\\,362\\,448$ is *not* divisible by $3.$ Also, since it is not divisible by $3,$ it cannot be divisible by $9$ either.\n\n- The sum of the digits of $68\\,543\\,031$ is\n\n$$\n6+8+5+4+3+0+3+1 = 30,\n$$\n\nwhich is divisible by $3,$ but not divisible $9.$ So, $68\\,543\\,032$ is divisible by $3,$ but it is not divisible by $9.$\n\nTherefore, only I is divisible by $3$ and $9.$" 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186171757 1071 "Which digits, when inserted into the blank, make the number $93\\,455\\,13\\square$ divisible by $6$?\n\n1. $3$\n2. $6$\n3. $8$" 13194139534687 17592186045417 false] [17592186171757 1071 "Which digits, when inserted into the blank, make the number $93\\,455\\,13\\square$ divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $3$\n\nII. $6$\n\nIII. $8$" 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186171757 1074 "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3$. Remember that to determine whether a number is divisible by $3$, we check whether the sum of the digits is divisible by $3$.\n\nFor the given number, the sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n9+3+4+5+5+1+3+\\square = 30+\\square\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each of the given digits we can substitute into the blank.\n\n- If we substitute ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ into the blank, we get $93\\,455\\,13{\\color{blue}{3}}$, which is not divisible by $2$. Therefore, it is not divisible by $6$ either.\n- If we substitute ${\\color{blue}{6}}$ into the blank, we get $93\\,455\\,13{\\color{blue}{6}}$, which is divisible by $2$. The sum of the digits is $30 + {\\color{blue}{6}} = 36$, which is divisible by $3$, so the number is also divisible by $3$. Therefore, the number is divisible by $6$.\n- If we substitute ${\\color{blue}{8}}$ into the blank, we get $93\\,455\\,13{\\color{blue}{8}}$, which is divisible by $2$. However, it is not divisible by $3$ because the sum of the digits is $30+{\\color{blue}{8}}=38$, which is not divisible by $3$. So, the number is *not* divisible by $6$ either.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534687 17592186045417 false] [17592186171757 1074 "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$ Remember that to determine whether a number is divisible by $3,$ we check whether the sum of the digits is divisible by $3.$\n\nFor the given number, the sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n9+3+4+5+5+1+3+\\square = 30+\\square.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each of the given digits we can substitute into the blank.\n\n- If we substitute ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ into the blank, we get $93\\,455\\,13{\\color{blue}{3}},$ which is not divisible by $2.$ Therefore, it is not divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- If we substitute ${\\color{blue}{6}}$ into the blank, we get $93\\,455\\,13{\\color{blue}{6}},$ which is divisible by $2.$ The sum of the digits is $30 + {\\color{blue}{6}} = 36,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\n- If we substitute ${\\color{blue}{8}}$ into the blank, we get $93\\,455\\,13{\\color{blue}{8}},$ which is divisible by $2.$ However, it is not divisible by $3$ because the sum of the digits is $30+{\\color{blue}{8}}=38,$ which is not divisible by $3.$ So, the number is *not* divisible by $6$ either.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186171758 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $7$?\n\n1. $637$\n2. $368$\n3. $112$" 13194139534687 17592186045417 false] [17592186171758 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $7?$\n\nI. $637$\n\nII. $368$\n\nIII. $112$" 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186171758 1074 "To check if a number is divisible by $7$, double the last digit, subtract the result from the remaining digits, and then check if the difference is divisible by $7$.\n\n- For the number ${\\color{blue}{63}}{\\color{red}{7}}$, we have that\n${\\color{blue}{63}}-2({\\color{red}{7}})=63-14=49$\nwhich is divisible by $7$. Therefore, $637$ is divisible by $7$.\n- For the number ${\\color{blue}{36}}{\\color{red}{8}}$, we have that\n${\\color{blue}{36}}-2({\\color{red}{8}})=36-16=20$\nwhich is not divisible by $7$. Therefore, $368$ is not divisible by $7$.\n- For the number ${\\color{blue}{11}}{\\color{red}{2}}$, we have that\n${\\color{blue}{11}}-2({\\color{red}{2}})=11-4=7$\nwhich is divisible by $7$. Therefore, $112$ is divisible by $7$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534687 17592186045417 false] [17592186171758 1074 "To check if a number is divisible by $7,$ double the last digit, subtract the result from the remaining digits, and then check if the difference is divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number ${\\color{blue}{63}}{\\color{red}{7}},$ we have that \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{63}}-2({\\color{red}{7}})=63-14=49\n$$\n\nwhich is divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $637$ is divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number ${\\color{blue}{36}}{\\color{red}{8}},$ we have that \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{36}}-2({\\color{red}{8}})=36-16=20\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $368$ is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number ${\\color{blue}{11}}{\\color{red}{2}},$ we have that \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{11}}-2({\\color{red}{2}})=11-4=7\n$$\n\nwhich is divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $112$ is divisible by $7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186171932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432788 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186171932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432792 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186171932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432793 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186171932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432794 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186171933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432783 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186171933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432785 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186171933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432786 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186