> *A number is divisible by $3$ if the sum of its digits is divisible by $3$.*\n\nFor example, let's determine if the following number is divisible by $3{:}$\n\n$312\\,561$\nIf we sum the digits, we get\n\n$$\n3+1+2+5+6+1 = 18\n$$\n\nNow, since $18$ is divisible by $3$, we conclude that $312\\,561$ is divisible by $3$.\n\nThe rule for determining if a number is divisible by $9$ is similar:\n\n> *A number is divisible by $9$ if the sum of its digits is divisible by $9$.*\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139534687 17592186045417 false] [17592186173299 1064 "In a previous lesson, we studied some rules for determining if an integer is divisible by $2,5,10,4,$ and $8.$ In this lesson, we'll extend our understanding of divisibility rules to include $3,9,6,$ and $7.$\n\nWe have the following rule for determining if a number is divisible by $3{:}$\n\n*A number is divisible by $3$ if the sum of its digits is divisible by $3.$*\n\nFor example, let's determine if the following number is divisible by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n312\\,561\n$$\n\nIf we sum the digits, we get\n\n$$\n3+1+2+5+6+1 = 18.\n$$\n\nNow, since $18$ is divisible by $3,$ we conclude that $312\\,561$ is divisible by $3.$\n\nThe rule for determining if a number is divisible by $9$ is similar:\n\n*A number is divisible by $9$ if the sum of its digits is divisible by $9.$*\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186173300 1064 "The rule for dividing by $7$ is the trickiest rule of the ones we've studied.\n\n*To check if a number is divisible by $7,$ we double the last digit, subtract the result from the remaining digits, and then check if the difference is divisible by $7.$*\n\nFor example, let's check if the following number is divisible by $7{:}$\n\n$$\n46{\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nThe last digit is ${\\color{red}{2}}.$ If we double this digit, we get $2\\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} =  {\\color{blue}{4}}.$ Then, if we subtract ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ from the remaining digits, we get\n\n$$\n46 - {\\color{blue}{4}} = 42.\n$$\n\nNow, since $42$ is divisible by $7,$ we conclude that $462$ is divisible by $7.$\n\nLet's see some more examples." 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186173300 1064 "The rule for dividing by $7$ is the trickiest rule of the ones we've studied.\n\n> *To check if a number is divisible by $7$, we double the last digit, subtract the result from the remaining digits, and then check if the difference is divisible by $7$.*\n\nFor example, let's check if the following number is divisible by $7{:}$\n\n$$\n46{\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nThe last digit is ${\\color{red}{2}}$. If we double this digit, we get\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} = {\\color{blue}{4}}\n$$\n\nThen, if we subtract ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ from the remaining digits, we get\n\n$$\n46 - {\\color{blue}{4}} = 42\n$$\n\nNow, since $42$ is divisible by $7$, we conclude that $462$ is divisible by $7$.\n\nLet's see some more examples." 13194139534687 17592186045417 false] [17592186173304 1064 "We have the following rule for determining if a number is divisible by $6{:}$\n\n*A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$*\n\nFor example, let's determine if the following number is divisible by $6{:}$\n\n$$\n353,856\n$$\n\nTo check whether this number is divisible by $6,$ we need to check that it's divisible by *both* $2$ *and* $3{:}$\n\n- Notice that the last digit of $353,85{\\color{blue}{6}}$ is ${\\color{blue}{6}}.$ Since ${\\color{blue}{6}}$ is even, $353,856$ is divisible by $2.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- We now check for divisibility by $3.$ The sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n3+5+3+8+5+6 = 30.\n$$\n\nSince $30$ is divisible by $3,$ we have that $353,856$ is divisible by $3.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nSo, since $353,856$ is divisible by *both* $2$ *and* $3,$ we conclude that it is divisible by $6.$\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186173304 1064 "We have the following rule for determining if a number is divisible by $6{:}$\n\n> *A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3$.*\n\nFor example, let's determine if the following number is divisible by $6{:}$\n\n$$\n353,856\n$$\n\nTo check whether this number is divisible by $6$, we need to check that it's divisible by *both* $2$ *and* $3{:}$\n\n- Notice that the last digit of $353,85{\\color{blue}{6}}$ is ${\\color{blue}{6}}$. Since ${\\color{blue}{6}}$ is even, $353,856$ is divisible by $2.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- We now check for divisibility by $3$. The sum of the digits is\n$3+5+3+8+5+6 = 30$.\nSince $30$ is divisible by $3$, we have that $353,856$ is divisible by $3.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nSo, since $353,856$ is divisible by *both* $2$ *and* $3$, we conclude that it is divisible by $6$.\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139534687 17592186045417 false] [17592186432699 1080 "II and III only" 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186432699 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186432699 1083 #uuid "0120a1f8-aa48-5ed0-8305-31cbb2b1f611" 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186432700 1080 "II only" 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186432700 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186432700 1083 #uuid "023e8ca5-d704-5d99-b064-aeb77c93d254" 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186432701 1080 "II and III only" 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186432701 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186432701 1083 #uuid "031e87af-b7df-5caa-98ff-dd4bdf5b2596" 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186432702 1080 "I and III only" 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186432702 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186432702 1083 #uuid "03716efb-8dd0-5692-a7ad-ddc54290f97c" 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186432703 1080 "I and II only" 13194139534687 17592186045417 true] [17592186432703 1082 #edb/re